1
00:00:01,280 --> 00:00:09,510
now that we have discussed memory virtualization
let's briefly discuss I/O virtualization

2
00:00:09,510 --> 00:00:18,670
so in general the hypervisor so here we are assuming
now it is directly working on top of the

3
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:25,100
hardware probes the devices at start up similarly
to what their operating system does when there

4
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:27,050
is no virtualization

5
00:00:27,050 --> 00:00:33,640
and once this is done it can report them to
the different operating systems the guest operating

6
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:39,809
systems that it is running and of course we can
use the interrupt mechanism to make sure

7
00:00:39,809 --> 00:00:45,579
the hypervisor wakes up and does that
necessarily emulation when each guest operating

8
00:00:45,579 --> 00:00:48,899
system is trying to interact with a device

9
00:00:48,899 --> 00:00:54,190
now for some devices of course you realize
the operating system cannot really take control

10
00:00:54,190 --> 00:00:59,860
of the entire device so if you have one disk
and that is being shared with all the operating

11
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:04,460
systems you cannot really let the operating
system take full control of the disk because

12
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:07,810
parts of that disk belong to other or are accessed
by

13
00:01:07,810 --> 00:01:15,409
other guest operating systems so for some devices
actually with virtualization what

14
00:01:15,409 --> 00:01:22,200
you do is that you emulate the device in
different ways so for a disk for example you

15
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:30,250
actually create a file that is maintained
by the hypervisor and that is what is actually

16
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:34,790
used to store what the guest operating system
believes is storing on

17
00:01:34,790 --> 00:01:40,090
disk it's beneficial because for example you
can initially create a small file and let

18
00:01:40,090 --> 00:01:45,130
the file grow depending on what is being written
there by the guest operating system rather

19
00:01:45,130 --> 00:01:53,070
than allocating right from the start the entire
space that the guest operating system

20
00:01:53,070 --> 00:01:56,350
believe it has access to in the disk

21
00:01:56,350 --> 00:02:01,450
when it comes to a network device for example
then you could have the emulation performed

22
00:02:01,450 --> 00:02:07,790
by the hypervisor by actually having the
hypervisor acting as a switch so each guest

23
00:02:07,790 --> 00:02:14,740
operating system can have a different
mac address and then the way in which the

24
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:19,330
hypervisor handles different requests
for the network card is by actually behaving

25
00:02:19,330 --> 00:02:27,959
like a regular switch also for virtualization
of I/O operations we have here the hardware

26
00:02:27,959 --> 00:02:36,870
coming to the rescue of the software
because as we mentioned several times at this

27
00:02:36,870 --> 00:02:41,680
point sometimes it's beneficial to let the
guest operating system and the processes run

28
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:46,250
by having the guest operating system interact directly
with the hardware rather than

29
00:02:46,250 --> 00:02:56,340
having a sort of hypervisor-in-the-middle setup
so this is supported by modern hardware through

30
00:02:56,340 --> 00:03:05,240
this thing called single route I/O virtualization 
which basically is provided

31
00:03:05,240 --> 00:03:12,790
by having at the hardware level different
memory spaces different interrupts reserved

32
00:03:12,790 --> 00:03:20,090
for each different virtual operating system
guest virtual machine

33
00:03:20,090 --> 00:03:25,870
so basically each virtual machine can map 
his own memory to the corresponding portion

34
00:03:25,870 --> 00:03:34,260
of memory you have in the physical hardware and
then you can handle the different I/O

35
00:03:34,260 --> 00:03:40,510
requests coming from different guest operating
systems directly at the hardware level

